returns "Wednesday" in O(1) time ``` ### The "Aha!" Moment That `days[3]` example above? **That is exactly how a Hash Table works.** A Hash Table is essentially just an array. But instead of *you* having to know that "Wednesday" is at index 3, the Hash Table uses a math equation (the hash function) to figure out the index for you. When you ask a Hash Table to find "Bob", it doesn't loop. It runs "Bob" through a math equation, the equation spits out the number `42`, and then the Hash Table does an O(1) array access: `memory_array[42]`. ### If Hash Tables are so fast, why do we use Arrays and loop through them? If searching an array is O(n), and searching a hash table is O(1), why do we even use arrays? Why not use hash tables for everything? 1. **Order matters:** Arrays keep things in the exact order you put them in. Hash tables scramble things up. If you need to display the top 10 high scores in order, a hash table is useless. 2. **You don't always have a "Key":** If you want to render a list of 50 blog posts on a screen, you aren't looking for one specific post. You just need to loop through all of them to draw them on the screen. Arrays are perfect for this. 3. **Filtering:** If you want to find *all* users who are over the age of 18, a hash table can't really help you. You have to loop through everyone anyway. **Summary:** Your real-world experience is entirely valid. You are almost always doing O(n) searches when you use arrays. But the underlying physical structure of the array allows for O(1) *index access*—and it's that exact physical property that Hash Tables exploit to do their magic.
